{"id": "Q01", "type_label": "Paraphrased lookup", "kind": "paraphrased_lookup", "profile": "lookup", "expected_tools": ["lookup_fact", "explore_documents"], "question": "How well does the custom-built neural network spot tea leaf diseases, compared with the transfer-learning model used in the same study?", "expected_answer": "The custom CNN built from scratch reached 94% overall accuracy, beating the ResNet50V2 transfer-learning model in the same study, which reached 84% accuracy.", "documents": ["GreenGuard_Mobile_Application_for_Real-Time_Tea_Leaves_Disease_Detection_and_Treatment_Guidance.pdf"], "answerable": true, "notes": "Wording deliberately avoids the document's own phrase 'custom CNN' (uses 'custom-built neural network') and 'accuracy' (uses 'spot'). Section VI, p.5.", "source": {"id": "Q01", "type": "Paraphrased lookup", "question": "How well does the custom-built neural network spot tea leaf diseases, compared with the transfer-learning model used in the same study?", "expected answer": "The custom CNN built from scratch reached 94% overall accuracy, beating the ResNet50V2 transfer-learning model in the same study, which reached 84% accuracy.", "document": "GreenGuard_Mobile_Application_for_Real-Time_Tea_Leaves_Disease_Detection_and_Treatment_Guidance.pdf", "exact quote": "\"The CNN model built from scratch achieved the highest overall accuracy of 94% in classifying tea leaf diseases.\" / \"The ResNet50V2 architecture achieved 84% accuracy in disease classification, which is higher than the MobileNetV2.\"", "other acceptable quote (optional)": "\"The custom CNN model surpasses the ResNet50V2 model's accuracy while achieving 94% accuracy in disease detection.\" (Abstract)", "must include": "94%, 84%, ResNet50V2", "notes": "Wording deliberately avoids the document's own phrase 'custom CNN' (uses 'custom-built neural network') and 'accuracy' (uses 'spot'). Section VI, p.5."}, "expected_quote": "overall accuracy of 94% in classifying tea leaf diseases", "also_required": ["84% accuracy in disease classification, which is higher than"], "also_acceptable": ["The custom CNN model surpasses the ResNet50V2 model's accuracy while achieving 94% accuracy in disease detection."], "must_include": ["94%", "84%", "ResNet50V2"], "quote_adjustments": [{"field": "expected_quote", "original": "The CNN model built from scratch achieved the highest overall accuracy of 94% in classifying tea leaf diseases.", "used": "overall accuracy of 94% in classifying tea leaf diseases", "reason": "Two-column PDF: extraction interleaved the columns mid-sentence ('pre' + this text + 'cision'), so only this contiguous part exists in the extracted text."}, {"field": "also_required", "original": "The ResNet50V2 architecture achieved 84% accuracy in disease classification, which is higher than the MobileNetV2.", "used": "84% accuracy in disease classification, which is higher than", "reason": "Two-column PDF: extraction interleaves 'The ResNet50V2 architecture achieved' with the other column before '84% accuracy...', so only this contiguous part exists."}]}
{"id": "Q02", "type_label": "Paraphrased lookup", "kind": "paraphrased_lookup", "profile": "lookup", "expected_tools": ["lookup_fact", "explore_documents"], "question": "How does PowerProx decide what each person using the system is allowed to do?", "expected_answer": "Through Role-Based Access Control (RBAC): every user is assigned an access level or role (e.g. Administrator, Engineer/Technical User, Technician, Approving Officer/Manager, Viewer/Read-Only User) and that role determines which modules, functions and actions they can use.", "documents": ["SLT_PowerProx_solution_architecture.docx"], "answerable": true, "notes": "Section 6.2 'Access Control Model'. Paraphrase swaps 'manage user permissions' for 'decide what... allowed to do'.", "source": {"id": "Q02", "type": "Paraphrased lookup", "question": "How does PowerProx decide what each person using the system is allowed to do?", "expected answer": "Through Role-Based Access Control (RBAC): every user is assigned an access level or role (e.g. Administrator, Engineer/Technical User, Technician, Approving Officer/Manager, Viewer/Read-Only User) and that role determines which modules, functions and actions they can use.", "document": "SLT_PowerProx_solution_architecture.docx", "exact quote": "\"PowerProx uses a Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) approach to manage user permissions. Each user is assigned an access level or role that determines the modules, functions, and actions available to that user.\"", "other acceptable quote (optional)": null, "must include": "Role-Based Access Control / RBAC", "notes": "Section 6.2 'Access Control Model'. Paraphrase swaps 'manage user permissions' for 'decide what... allowed to do'."}, "expected_quote": "PowerProx uses a Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) approach to manage user permissions. Each user is assigned an access level or role that determines the modules, functions, and actions available to that user.", "also_required": [], "must_include": ["Role-Based Access Control|RBAC"]}
{"id": "Q03", "type_label": "Paraphrased lookup", "kind": "paraphrased_lookup", "profile": "lookup", "expected_tools": ["lookup_fact"], "question": "In the SmartSmile app, how long does the phone have to come back with a result after someone scans an X-ray?", "expected_answer": "Within 5 seconds.", "documents": ["Group_27_-_report_-_SmartSmile_edgeAI.pdf"], "answerable": true, "notes": "Functional requirements, FR3 'On-Device Inference', p.7-8. Paraphrase swaps 'complete inference' for 'come back with a result'.", "source": {"id": "Q03", "type": "Paraphrased lookup", "question": "In the SmartSmile app, how long does the phone have to come back with a result after someone scans an X-ray?", "expected answer": "Within 5 seconds.", "document": "Group_27_-_report_-_SmartSmile_edgeAI.pdf", "exact quote": "\"FR3.5: The system shall complete inference within 5 seconds on target devices.\"", "other acceptable quote (optional)": null, "must include": "5 seconds", "notes": "Functional requirements, FR3 'On-Device Inference', p.7-8. Paraphrase swaps 'complete inference' for 'come back with a result'."}, "expected_quote": "FR3.5: The system shall complete inference within 5 seconds on target devices.", "also_required": [], "must_include": ["5 seconds"]}
{"id": "Q04", "type_label": "Exact-term lookup", "kind": "exact_term", "profile": "lookup", "expected_tools": ["lookup_fact"], "question": "What is the name of the API endpoint that handles CV ranking requests in the CV Filtering System?", "expected_answer": "/rank_cvs", "documents": ["CV_Filtering_System_AI_.docx"], "answerable": true, "notes": "Exact keyword/code lookup; endpoint name given verbatim in the document.", "source": {"id": "Q04", "type": "Exact-term lookup", "question": "What is the name of the API endpoint that handles CV ranking requests in the CV Filtering System?", "expected answer": "/rank_cvs", "document": "CV_Filtering_System_AI_.docx", "exact quote": "\"3. API Endpoint (/rank_cvs) - Receives filtering request with custom prompt\"", "other acceptable quote (optional)": null, "must include": "/rank_cvs", "notes": "Exact keyword/code lookup; endpoint name given verbatim in the document."}, "expected_quote": "API Endpoint (/rank_cvs) Receives filtering request with custom prompt", "also_required": [], "must_include": ["/rank_cvs"], "quote_adjustments": [{"field": "expected_quote", "original": "3. API Endpoint (/rank_cvs) - Receives filtering request with custom prompt", "used": "API Endpoint (/rank_cvs) Receives filtering request with custom prompt", "reason": "Word list formatting: the ' - ' between the endpoint and 'Receives' is not in the extracted text."}]}
{"id": "Q05", "type_label": "Exact-term lookup", "kind": "exact_term", "profile": "lookup", "expected_tools": ["lookup_fact"], "question": "Which port number does MySQL use in Server B's firewall rule in the SLT server migration proposal?", "expected_answer": "3306", "documents": ["SLT_Server_Migration_Proposal.docx"], "answerable": true, "notes": "Section 7 'Security Considerations'. 5432 (PostgreSQL's port) sits in the same sentence as a close distractor.", "source": {"id": "Q05", "type": "Exact-term lookup", "question": "Which port number does MySQL use in Server B's firewall rule in the SLT server migration proposal?", "expected answer": "3306", "document": "SLT_Server_Migration_Proposal.docx", "exact quote": "\"Server B (database tier) is firewalled to accept traffic only from Server A, and only on database ports (5432 for PostgreSQL, 3306 for MySQL)\"", "other acceptable quote (optional)": null, "must include": "3306", "notes": "Section 7 'Security Considerations'. 5432 (PostgreSQL's port) sits in the same sentence as a close distractor."}, "expected_quote": "Server B (database tier) is firewalled to accept traffic only from Server A, and only on database ports (5432 for PostgreSQL, 3306 for MySQL)", "also_required": [], "must_include": ["3306"]}
{"id": "Q06", "type_label": "Table value", "kind": "table", "profile": "lookup", "expected_tools": ["lookup_fact"], "question": "In the GreenGuard classification report (Table I), what F1-score and support count did the 'Gray light' disease class get?", "expected_answer": "F1-score 0.98, with a support (sample count) of 23.", "documents": ["GreenGuard_Mobile_Application_for_Real-Time_Tea_Leaves_Disease_Detection_and_Treatment_Guidance.pdf"], "answerable": true, "notes": "Answer is a table cell, not stated in prose anywhere else. Table I, Section V, p.4.", "source": {"id": "Q06", "type": "Table value", "question": "In the GreenGuard classification report (Table I), what F1-score and support count did the 'Gray light' disease class get?", "expected answer": "F1-score 0.98, with a support (sample count) of 23.", "document": "GreenGuard_Mobile_Application_for_Real-Time_Tea_Leaves_Disease_Detection_and_Treatment_Guidance.pdf", "exact quote": "\"Gray light 0.96 1.00 0.98 23\" (row of TABLE I: CLASSIFICATION REPORT, columns Precision/Recall/F1-score/Support)", "other acceptable quote (optional)": null, "must include": "0.98, 23", "notes": "Answer is a table cell, not stated in prose anywhere else. Table I, Section V, p.4."}, "expected_quote": "Gray light 0.96 1.00 0.98 23", "also_required": [], "must_include": ["0.98", "23"]}
{"id": "Q07", "type_label": "Table value", "kind": "table", "profile": "lookup", "expected_tools": ["lookup_fact"], "question": "How much RAM is proposed for Server B (the database server) in the starting sizing allocation of the SLT server migration proposal?", "expected_answer": "4-8 GB.", "documents": ["SLT_Server_Migration_Proposal.docx"], "answerable": true, "notes": "Look-alike distractor sits one row above: Server A (Web/App) is listed at a flat 4 GB, not a range.", "source": {"id": "Q07", "type": "Table value", "question": "How much RAM is proposed for Server B (the database server) in the starting sizing allocation of the SLT server migration proposal?", "expected answer": "4-8 GB.", "document": "SLT_Server_Migration_Proposal.docx", "exact quote": "\"| Server B (Database) | 2 | 4-8 GB | SSD (priority) | Postgres + MySQL, separate data volumes if possible |\" (Section 5, Server Sizing table)", "other acceptable quote (optional)": null, "must include": "4-8 GB", "notes": "Look-alike distractor sits one row above: Server A (Web/App) is listed at a flat 4 GB, not a range."}, "expected_quote": "| Server B (Database) | 2 | 4-8 GB | SSD (priority) | Postgres + MySQL, separate data volumes if possible |", "also_required": [], "must_include": ["4-8 GB|4 to 8 GB"]}
{"id": "Q08", "type_label": "Multi-passage (one document)", "kind": "multi_passage", "profile": "explore", "expected_tools": ["explore_documents", "lookup_fact"], "question": "In CM4607, which fitness-function component was given the highest weight, and does the GA's actual final result reflect that priority?", "expected_answer": "Average waiting time (Wavg) has the highest weight, w1 = 100, described as the 'Primary objective' in the weight-justification section. The final result reflects that priority: the GA achieved a 51.3% reduction in average waiting time, and the discussion explicitly accepts a slightly worse maximum queue (14 to 15 vehicles) as 'an acceptable trade-off' for that waiting-time gain.", "documents": ["CM4607_Esandu_Report.pdf"], "answerable": true, "notes": "Requires combining Section 3.4.3 (Weight Justification) with Section 4.2.1 (Results and Discussion) — two different parts of the same report.", "source": {"id": "Q08", "type": "Multi-passage (one document)", "question": "In CM4607, which fitness-function component was given the highest weight, and does the GA's actual final result reflect that priority?", "expected answer": "Average waiting time (Wavg) has the highest weight, w1 = 100, described as the 'Primary objective' in the weight-justification section. The final result reflects that priority: the GA achieved a 51.3% reduction in average waiting time, and the discussion explicitly accepts a slightly worse maximum queue (14 to 15 vehicles) as 'an acceptable trade-off' for that waiting-time gain.", "document": "CM4607_Esandu_Report.pdf", "exact quote": "\"w1 = 100 (High): Primary objective. Waiting time directly impacts user experience and is the most critical metric in traffic engineering.\" (Section 3.4.3) / \"maximum queue increased marginally from 14 to 15 vehicles, an acceptable trade-off given the 51% improvement in average waiting time.\" (Section 4.2.1)", "other acceptable quote (optional)": null, "must include": "w1 = 100, waiting time, 51.3% (or 51%), trade-off", "notes": "Requires combining Section 3.4.3 (Weight Justification) with Section 4.2.1 (Results and Discussion) — two different parts of the same report."}, "expected_quote": "w1 = 100 (High): Primary objective. Waiting time directly impacts user experience and is the most critical metric in traffic engineering.", "also_required": ["maximum queue increased marginally from 14 to 15 vehicles, an acceptable trade-off given the 51% improvement in average waiting time"], "must_include": ["w1 = 100|w1=100|w1 of 100", "waiting time", "51.3%|51%", "trade-off|tradeoff"], "quote_adjustments": [{"field": "also_required", "original": "maximum queue increased marginally from 14 to 15 vehicles, an acceptable trade-off given the 51% improvement in average waiting time.", "used": "maximum queue increased marginally from 14 to 15 vehicles, an acceptable trade-off given the 51% improvement in average waiting time", "reason": "The document reads 'waiting time).' - trailing period dropped so the parenthesis does not break the match."}]}
{"id": "Q09", "type_label": "Multi-passage (one document)", "kind": "multi_passage", "profile": "explore", "expected_tools": ["explore_documents", "lookup_fact"], "question": "Of the 250 posts used as the manually annotated sentiment test set in the NLP coursework report, how many did Esandu actually label himself versus have an LLM label?", "expected_answer": "He personally annotated 50 of the 250 posts, then had Gemini label the other 200 after checking that Gemini's labels matched his own 50.", "documents": ["NLP_CWReport_2237041.pdf"], "answerable": true, "notes": "Combines Task 5a (Part A, p.3) with the separate 'Use of Generative AI' section (p.5) later in the same report.", "source": {"id": "Q09", "type": "Multi-passage (one document)", "question": "Of the 250 posts used as the manually annotated sentiment test set in the NLP coursework report, how many did Esandu actually label himself versus have an LLM label?", "expected answer": "He personally annotated 50 of the 250 posts, then had Gemini label the other 200 after checking that Gemini's labels matched his own 50.", "document": "NLP_CWReport_2237041.pdf", "exact quote": "\"I randomly selected 250 posts for manual annotation as the test set.\" (Task 5a) / \"I first manually annotated 50 of them, and then gave that 50 to gemini to annotate. After comparing my annotations and the llms annotations, since it was accurate, I got the remaining 200 annotated using the llm.\" (Use of Generative AI, item 5)", "other acceptable quote (optional)": null, "must include": "50, 200, Gemini", "notes": "Combines Task 5a (Part A, p.3) with the separate 'Use of Generative AI' section (p.5) later in the same report."}, "expected_quote": "I randomly selected 250 posts for manual annotation as the test set.", "also_required": ["I first manually annotated 50 of them, and then gave that 50 to gemini to annotate. After comparing my annotations and the llms annotations, since it was accurate, I got the remaining 200 annotated using the llm."], "must_include": ["50", "200", "Gemini"]}
{"id": "Q10", "type_label": "Cross-document", "kind": "cross_doc", "profile": "explore", "expected_tools": ["explore_documents", "lookup_fact"], "question": "Esandu's individual CM4607 report says the GA achieved a 51.3% reduction in average waiting time versus baseline. How does that compare to the improvement DQN achieved over fixed-time control in the medium-traffic scenario reviewed in the group's RL literature review (rt-grp-1-report)?", "expected_answer": "They are in the same ballpark: CM4607's own GA cut average waiting time by 51.3% versus baseline. In the group report's review of Mousavi et al.'s medium-traffic scenario (Table 4), DQN and DDPG achieved a 47-49% reduction in waiting time versus fixed-time control (94.8s/91.5s vs a 180.2s baseline). So Esandu's own GA result slightly exceeds the DQN figure reported in the literature review.", "documents": ["CM4607_Esandu_Report.pdf", "rt-grp-1-report.pdf"], "answerable": true, "notes": "CM4607 is Esandu's own individual coursework; rt-grp-1-report is a separate group literature-review document. Both concern traffic-signal RL/GA but are different files.", "source": {"id": "Q10", "type": "Cross-document", "question": "Esandu's individual CM4607 report says the GA achieved a 51.3% reduction in average waiting time versus baseline. How does that compare to the improvement DQN achieved over fixed-time control in the medium-traffic scenario reviewed in the group's RL literature review (rt-grp-1-report)?", "expected answer": "They are in the same ballpark: CM4607's own GA cut average waiting time by 51.3% versus baseline. In the group report's review of Mousavi et al.'s medium-traffic scenario (Table 4), DQN and DDPG achieved a 47-49% reduction in waiting time versus fixed-time control (94.8s/91.5s vs a 180.2s baseline). So Esandu's own GA result slightly exceeds the DQN figure reported in the literature review.", "document": "CM4607_Esandu_Report.pdf; rt-grp-1-report.pdf", "exact quote": "CM4607: \"51.3% reduction in average waiting time (3.011s -> 1.468s)\" (Section 4.2.1) | rt-grp-1-report: \"DQN and DDPG achieve 47-49% reduction in waiting time versus fixed-time control\" (Section 5.3.4, Table 4 discussion)", "other acceptable quote (optional)": null, "must include": "51.3%, 47-49%", "notes": "CM4607 is Esandu's own individual coursework; rt-grp-1-report is a separate group literature-review document. Both concern traffic-signal RL/GA but are different files."}, "expected_quote": "51.3% reduction in average waiting time", "also_required": ["Substantial improvement over conventional methods: DQN and DDPG achieve"], "must_include": ["51.3%", "47-49%|47 to 49%|47% to 49%"], "quote_adjustments": [{"field": "expected_quote", "original": "51.3% reduction in average waiting time (3.011s -> 1.468s)", "used": "51.3% reduction in average waiting time", "reason": "Spacing: the document reads '->1.468s', the quote '-> 1.468s'; the numeric tail is dropped, the claim is kept."}, {"field": "also_required", "original": "DQN and DDPG achieve 47-49% reduction in waiting time versus fixed-time control", "used": "Substantial improvement over conventional methods: DQN and DDPG achieve", "reason": "PDF extraction dropped the en dash: the document's '47–49%' is stored as '4749%', so the quote stops before the number."}], "facts_lost_in_extraction": ["47-49%|47 to 49%|47% to 49%"]}
{"id": "Q11", "type_label": "Look-alike trap", "kind": "lookalike_trap", "profile": "lookup", "expected_tools": ["lookup_fact"], "question": "In the GreenGuard paper's SOTA comparison table (Table II), what accuracy figure is reported for Xie et al.'s Swin-Transformer-based method on the Chaozhou Dancong tea dataset?", "expected_answer": "94% (reported as 'Detection accuracy').", "documents": ["GreenGuard_Mobile_Application_for_Real-Time_Tea_Leaves_Disease_Detection_and_Treatment_Guidance.pdf"], "answerable": true, "notes": "Trap: '94%' appears three times in Table II for three different rows/metrics - Xie et al.'s detection accuracy, Hu & Fang's MergeModel precision, and the paper's own proposed CNN accuracy. Question names the author so only one row is correct.", "source": {"id": "Q11", "type": "Look-alike trap", "question": "In the GreenGuard paper's SOTA comparison table (Table II), what accuracy figure is reported for Xie et al.'s Swin-Transformer-based method on the Chaozhou Dancong tea dataset?", "expected answer": "94% (reported as 'Detection accuracy').", "document": "GreenGuard_Mobile_Application_for_Real-Time_Tea_Leaves_Disease_Detection_and_Treatment_Guidance.pdf", "exact quote": "\"Xie et al. [1] Chaozhou Dancong Tea dataset (Self-made dataset) 5 Swim Transformer depth learning algorithm with migration learning Precision, Recall, Specificity Detection accuracy - 94%\" (Table II)", "other acceptable quote (optional)": null, "must include": "Xie et al., 94%", "notes": "Trap: '94%' appears three times in Table II for three different rows/metrics - Xie et al.'s detection accuracy, Hu & Fang's MergeModel precision, and the paper's own proposed CNN accuracy. Question names the author so only one row is correct."}, "expected_quote": "Xie et al. [1] Chaozhou Dancong Tea dataset (Self-made dataset) 5 Swim Transformer depth learning algorithm with migration learning", "also_required": [], "must_include": ["Xie", "94%"], "quote_adjustments": [{"field": "expected_quote", "original": "Xie et al. [1] Chaozhou Dancong Tea dataset (Self-made dataset) 5 Swim Transformer depth learning algorithm with migration learning Precision, Recall, Specificity Detection accuracy - 94%", "used": "Xie et al. [1] Chaozhou Dancong Tea dataset (Self-made dataset) 5 Swim Transformer depth learning algorithm with migration learning", "reason": "Table cell hyphenation: 'speci-<br>ficity' is split in the extracted table, so the quote stops before it. The Xie et al. row is still uniquely identified."}]}
{"id": "Q12", "type_label": "Look-alike trap", "kind": "lookalike_trap", "profile": "lookup", "expected_tools": ["lookup_fact"], "question": "According to the CM4608 feedback grid (the grading table, not the main task description), what subset size must an Excellent (Grade A) Task 1 submission clearly identify through EDA?", "expected_answer": "At least 15,000 - this is a different figure from the 50,000-entry minimum raw collection required in the main task description earlier in the same brief.", "documents": ["CM4608_Assessment_Brief_Coursework.pdf"], "answerable": true, "notes": "Trap: two similarly-worded dataset-size requirements (50,000 raw collection vs. 15,000 EDA subset) sit in different sections of the same document.", "source": {"id": "Q12", "type": "Look-alike trap", "question": "According to the CM4608 feedback grid (the grading table, not the main task description), what subset size must an Excellent (Grade A) Task 1 submission clearly identify through EDA?", "expected answer": "At least 15,000 - this is a different figure from the 50,000-entry minimum raw collection required in the main task description earlier in the same brief.", "document": "CM4608_Assessment_Brief_Coursework.pdf", "exact quote": "\"Collected a large dataset from which a diverse subset of at least 15,000 was clearly identified using carefully selected informative exploratory data analysis.\" (Feedback grid, Task 1 row, p.7)", "other acceptable quote (optional)": "\"Collect a diverse dataset of at least 50,000 user-generated text entries from the r/srilanka subreddit\" (Task 1a, main task description, p.2) - this is the distractor, not the answer", "must include": "15,000", "notes": "Trap: two similarly-worded dataset-size requirements (50,000 raw collection vs. 15,000 EDA subset) sit in different sections of the same document."}, "expected_quote": "Collected a large dataset from which a diverse subset of at least 15,000 was clearly identified using carefully selected informative exploratory data analysis.", "also_required": [], "distractor_quotes": ["Collect a diverse dataset of at least 50,000 user-generated text entries from the r/srilanka subreddit"], "must_include": ["15,000|15000"]}
{"id": "Q13", "type_label": "Conflicting versions", "kind": "conflicting_versions", "profile": "lookup", "expected_tools": ["lookup_fact", "explore_documents"], "question": "What crossover probability did the GA in CM4607 use?", "expected_answer": "The document actually gives two different figures for this. The two-point crossover operator is formally defined with Pc = 0.7 (70%), but the justification paragraph right after it, and the Table 4 methodology summary, both state 75% instead. A careful answer should flag this inconsistency and give both values, noting that 75% is the one repeated twice (justification text + summary table) versus 70% stated once (the formal definition).", "documents": ["CM4607_Esandu_Report.pdf"], "answerable": true, "notes": "Genuine in-document inconsistency, not an OCR artifact - confirmed by checking all three occurrences. Pass condition: answer surfaces both numbers rather than confidently picking just one.", "source": {"id": "Q13", "type": "Conflicting versions", "question": "What crossover probability did the GA in CM4607 use?", "expected answer": "The document actually gives two different figures for this. The two-point crossover operator is formally defined with Pc = 0.7 (70%), but the justification paragraph right after it, and the Table 4 methodology summary, both state 75% instead. A careful answer should flag this inconsistency and give both values, noting that 75% is the one repeated twice (justification text + summary table) versus 70% stated once (the formal definition).", "document": "CM4607_Esandu_Report.pdf", "exact quote": "\"Two-point crossover was applied with probability Pc = 0.7 (70%)\" (Section 3.6.2) / \"The crossover probability of 75% ensures that 25% of individuals pass to the next generation unchanged\" (Section 3.6.2, same subsection)", "other acceptable quote (optional)": "\"Crossover Two-point (75%)\" (Table 4, GA Methodology Components Summary, Section 3.7)", "must include": "70% AND 75% (both values must appear)", "notes": "Genuine in-document inconsistency, not an OCR artifact - confirmed by checking all three occurrences. Pass condition: answer surfaces both numbers rather than confidently picking just one."}, "expected_quote": "The crossover probability of 75% ensures that 25% of individuals pass to the next generation unchanged", "also_required": ["Two-point crossover was applied with probability Pc = 0.7 (70%)"], "also_acceptable": ["Crossover Two-point (75%)"], "must_include": ["70%|0.7", "75%|0.75"]}
{"id": "Q14", "type_label": "Summary", "kind": "summary", "profile": "summarize", "expected_tools": ["summarize_documents", "explore_documents"], "question": "Summarise how SmartSmile's model-retraining pipeline works, from a low-confidence X-ray detection to an updated model reaching users' phones.", "expected_answer": "A detection below the confidence threshold is flagged and the image is uploaded to S3 via a Lambda function. A dentist reviews and annotates it in Label Studio (hosted on Elastic Beanstalk). A nightly Lambda job syncs the new annotations back to S3 as training data. Once enough new labelled samples exist, an AWS EventBridge-triggered Lambda kicks off a SageMaker notebook that fine-tunes the YOLOv10 model and validates its performance. The updated model is then deployed and distributed to end devices in a future app version.", "documents": ["Group_27_-_report_-_SmartSmile_edgeAI.pdf"], "answerable": true, "notes": "Summary-mode question. Per the eval setup, summary mode can't be reranked on the free Voyage key, so retrieval is expected to be weaker here - report that honestly rather than treating a partially-grounded answer as an outright failure. Note: the document also gives two different retraining thresholds (100 vs 300 new samples) and two confidence thresholds (30% vs 50%) - a good summary shouldn't quietly pick one without flagging the mismatch.", "source": {"id": "Q14", "type": "Summary", "question": "Summarise how SmartSmile's model-retraining pipeline works, from a low-confidence X-ray detection to an updated model reaching users' phones.", "expected answer": "A detection below the confidence threshold is flagged and the image is uploaded to S3 via a Lambda function. A dentist reviews and annotates it in Label Studio (hosted on Elastic Beanstalk). A nightly Lambda job syncs the new annotations back to S3 as training data. Once enough new labelled samples exist, an AWS EventBridge-triggered Lambda kicks off a SageMaker notebook that fine-tunes the YOLOv10 model and validates its performance. The updated model is then deployed and distributed to end devices in a future app version.", "document": "Group_27_-_report_-_SmartSmile_edgeAI.pdf", "exact quote": "\"A nightly Lambda job syncs these to a training dataset.\" / \"AWS Event Bridge -> AWS Lambda: Triggers monthly execution of the lambda function\" / \"AWS SageMaker AI -> S3 Bucket: Stores updated model.\" (Workflow Sequence + Sequence Diagram sections, p.11-16)", "other acceptable quote (optional)": null, "must include": "Label Studio, S3, Lambda, SageMaker, EventBridge", "notes": "Summary-mode question. Per the eval setup, summary mode can't be reranked on the free Voyage key, so retrieval is expected to be weaker here - report that honestly rather than treating a partially-grounded answer as an outright failure. Note: the document also gives two different retraining thresholds (100 vs 300 new samples) and two confidence thresholds (30% vs 50%) - a good summary shouldn't quietly pick one without flagging the mismatch."}, "expected_quote": "A nightly Lambda job syncs these to a training dataset.", "also_required": ["AWS Event Bridge -> AWS Lambda: Triggers monthly execution of the lambda function", "AWS SageMaker AI -> S3 Bucket: Stores updated model."], "must_include": ["Label Studio", "S3", "Lambda", "SageMaker", "EventBridge|Event Bridge"]}
